      "id": 1,
      "text": "Founded as Smithville in 1792, the town was renamed Southport in 1887 to boost its status as a major shipping port.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southport%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Southport grew around Fort Johnston, erected in 1745 after Spanish privateer raids threatened the Cape Fear coast.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southport%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Nestled on the Cape Fear River and Atlantic Intracoastal Waterway, Southport offers picturesque coastal scenery and Southern hospitality.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.exploresouthportnc.gov/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Wilmington, Brunswick and Southern Railroad opened in 1911 but ceased operations in 1945 after revenue losses and truck competition.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Southport%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Southport hosts North Carolina’s Fourth of July Festival, drawing 40,000 to 50,000 visitors each year.",
